Token Efficiency: Research, Brainstorm, and Enforcement Architecture
0. The core principle: deterministic gate before every metered model call
The strongest token-efficiency lever is not making models cheaper — it is not calling a model at all when a deterministic mechanism can produce or verify the result. Every model invocation should pass a pre-model deterministic gate:


Gate check	What must happen before any model call
Is this request already answerable from exact-match cache?	Return cached result.
Is there a semantic cache hit above a calibrated threshold?	Return cached result with provenance.
Can a rule engine, regex cascade, schema validator, SQL query, search index, or static tool resolve it?	Execute deterministically; do not invoke a model.
Is this a workflow step with a known deterministic procedure?	Run the blueprint; model is not allowed to choose the path.
Is the required context already resident in a prompt-cache prefix?	Reuse the prefix; never re-send identical tokens.
Is the token budget exhausted for this task class?	Stop with an explicit budget-exhausted error, not a silent truncation.
Only after all deterministic options are exhausted: invoke the smallest/cheapest model that can pass verification.	Log the escalation reason and the deterministic options attempted.
This is supported by the deterministic-first literature: the “Blueprint First, Model Second” framework codifies the workflow into an execution blueprint run by a deterministic engine, invoking the LLM only for bounded sub-tasks and never to decide the workflow path. On TravelPlanner it improved execution efficiency by 27.1% while cutting constraint violations by 96.0%. The runtime-loop pattern is the same: the framework acts first and only calls the LLM for what genuinely requires judgment. Static tool execution can run tools automatically before the model processes the request, rather than letting the model decide whether tools are needed. A pre-invocation cost gate can even use a regex cascade and historical success rates to skip LLM calls entirely when predicted success is below a configurable threshold.
The estate principle from your previous document — anything that can be machine-done must be done before using a model that costs money — should therefore be implemented as an enforced deterministic gate, not a guideline.

