Mathematical enforcement: the gateway must enforce tokens_consumed ≤ B_task with an explicit strict vs advisory semantics. Under strict mode, the engine stops with an explicit budget-exhausted error rather than emitting an invalid or truncated token stream. The founder can choose advisory mode for task classes where finishing the current attempt is worth the overshoot; advisory mode must still log the overshoot and charge it against a separate override budget.

L6 — Verification
Token efficiency and proof discipline are the same loop: do not pay for model calls whose outcomes cannot be verified. The estate should prefer model calls whose outputs are machine-checkable — schema validation, unit tests, formal verification, deterministic replay — over open-ended generative calls. Formal verification itself can be token-expensive (Anthropic reportedly spent over 100 million tokens on a formal-verification run), but when a task class is amenable to formal proof, the verification cost buys a durable guarantee that eliminates repeated model calls.

3. Mathematical enforcement architecture
3.1 Budget algebra
Define for every task class T:
text
B_T = B_det + B_cache + B_model_in + B_model_out + B_verify + B_retry
* B_det must be zero in model tokens. Deterministic work is CPU/tool cost, not token cost.
* B_cache is cache lookup and cache read cost; provider cache reads may be 0.1× base input rate.
* B_model_in and B_model_out are hard-capped at the gateway.
* B_retry is bounded by the expected number of verification iterations.
Enforced invariant:
text
for every completed task t in T:
    tokens_billed(t) ≤ B_T
OR
    task returns BudgetExhausted and no founder-facing claim is made
This mirrors the formal token-budget semantics where a depth cap restricts the language and the engine stops with an explicit error rather than emitting an invalid token.
3.2 Probabilistic bound for retries and escalation
For a verification or escalation loop with per-attempt success probability δ, the expected number of iterations is bounded by 4/δ, with worst-case outliers mathematically bounded and almost-sure termination proven. Use this as the formal basis for B_retry: set B_retry = max_attempts × max_tokens_per_attempt, with max_attempts derived from the calibrated δ for the task class. If δ degrades under distribution shift, the bound automatically tightens the retry budget or triggers recalibration.
3.3 Monte Carlo budget setting and outlier detection
Use historical sampling of completed runs and Monte Carlo simulation to project future token expenditure over a configurable time horizon. Set B_T at a chosen percentile (e.g., P95 or P99) of the simulated distribution. If actual expenditure exceeds the simulated bound more than a configured number of standard deviations, trip an alarm and require recalibration. Monte Carlo Tree Search can also jointly optimize workflow topology for performance, token consumption, latency, and failure risk. Agent coordination bounds can be validated with 50,000-run Monte Carlo simulation.
3.4 Cache mathematical guarantee
For semantic caching in continuous query space, the sublinear regret bound against an optimal oracle provides a formal performance guarantee. Enforce:
* Similarity threshold calibrated to a maximum accepted error rate among cached answers.
* Regret bound monitored; if the empirical regret exceeds the theoretical bound, the cache is invalidated or the threshold is tightened.
* Switching costs are optimized so that changing cached responses does not itself become a cost source.
3.5 Regression gates with a single efficiency metric
Use an Effective Tokens (ET) metric that weights output tokens at 4×, cache reads at 0.1×, then applies a model multiplier (e.g., small model 0.25×, mid 1.0×, frontier 5.0×) so a single number compares cost across models and catches regressions. Gate every prompt, model, tokenizer, router, or cache change:
text
ET_after ≤ ET_before × (1 + ε)
where ε is the founder-configurable regression tolerance (default: 0%). If the gate fails, the change is blocked unless a founder override is recorded with reason and expiry.
Also track:
* Regression-Run Efficiency (RRE): tokens an agent/CI reads to re-verify a known flow per run. A deterministic test suite can be ~175 tokens; an LLM re-drive can be ~30,249 tokens.
* Cost creep: normalized per-1K-token cost, not raw per-request cost, across providers and API changes.
* Escalation rate: percentage of tasks that reach a metered model after the deterministic gate.
* Machine-resolved rate: percentage of tasks resolved before any model call.
3.6 Calibration and confidence intervals
Routing and abstention thresholds must be calibrated, not heuristic. Uncertainty scores should be converted into risk-controlled selective-answering rules using confidence-interval-based calibration. The decision signal must be substantially cheaper than the action it avoids and calibrated near the deployment threshold.

