Your token-efficiency file is not a standalone optimization document; it is the economic engine of the entire estate. Viewed as a whole, it makes a coherent and forceful argument: efficiency is a control-plane problem, not a prompting problem. However, when placed alongside the proof, yield, and decision-making requirements from your broader architecture, several gaps and tensions become visible. The most consequential is that the document's crown jewel—Jev—is no longer a stable foundation; it is the opening move in a commodity race. The architecture needs to be decision-model-agnostic to survive what comes next.

✅ What the Whole Picture Gets Right
The document's core insight is correct and well-supported. The deterministic gate—"anything that can be machine-done must be done before using a model that costs money"—is not a heuristic; it is a control-plane primitive. KORA, a deterministic-first execution layer, demonstrates that a pre-inference control layer can avoid 80 of 100 model invocations on a deterministic-heavy benchmark with zero mismatches in the deterministic subset. The local-decider project proves the same principle in a tool-using agent context: "anything decidable by a rule is decided by a rule," with a measured 0-leak record on a 112-case safety probe. This is the strongest single lever in the entire document, and it is correctly positioned as the first gate, not an afterthought.
The budget algebra is also structurally sound. Defining B_T = B_det + B_cache + B_model_in + B_model_out + B_verify + B_retry and enforcing tokens_billed(t) ≤ B_T or BudgetExhausted gives the estate a checkable invariant, not a policy aspiration. This is exactly the discipline that production cost-governance gateways are now enforcing: costhelm projects every call at worst-case cost and admits it against a hard budget before any provider is contacted, returning an HTTP 402 with the arithmetic in the body and costing $0 because nothing was sent. Toka SDK provides hierarchical hard/soft limits across request, task, session, day, and month dimensions with explainable routing and zero silent degradation. CostGuard adds trajectory-level governance with a graduated compress-and-downgrade-then-terminate mitigation policy, achieving 33–82% savings over baselines with zero budget violations across tested ceilings—at the cost of a 16–21% quality reduction. The document's budget algebra is aligned with where the market is actually converging.
The distinction between strict and advisory budget semantics is also correct and necessary. A cumulative token cap can fall inside a derivation, forcing a choice between stopping at the cap and letting the current attempt finish; the two produce different costs and candidate pools, and budget curves must jointly state the cap, realized cost, stopping rule, and selector information. The document's insistence on explicit strict/advisory semantics, rather than silent truncation, is the right call.

⚠️ The Central Gap: Jev Is a Commodity, Not a Moat
The document treats Jev as "the default fast layer" and assigns it a Must priority as the bounded-decision resolver. That was defensible in September 2026. It is no longer defensible as a architectural assumption. Within weeks of Jev's launch, the decision-model category became a multi-player race: OpenAI launched a Decisions API with the Luna model, Cloudflare released Clef and Clef Flash (priced at $0.24 per million tokens), AWS open-sourced Strands Decider 2B, and Microsoft unveiled Microsoft-Decision-1. Cloudflare claims Clef is smarter and faster than Jev, with Clef Flash at roughly 39 ms median latency. Jev's own adoption is real—it reached nearly 13% of paid teams on Vercel's AI Gateway within 24 hours and became the default evaluation model for Vercel's agent framework—but the existence of multiple viable substitutes means Jev is not a durable architectural dependency.
More importantly, the evidence on Jev's reliability is mixed. TypeSafe's "0 per cent hallucination rate" claim refers to schema conformity, not decision correctness; independent experiments showed Jev's routing decisions could be influenced by misleading text, and confidence scores provided "some warning, but not a reliable safeguard against incorrect choices". Enterprise deployment requires labelled historical data, meaningful thresholds, and a clear understanding of false-positive versus false-negative costs—exactly the kind of validation data that most organizations lack. The document's own proof architecture acknowledges that a model cannot verify its own output as proof, yet Jev is positioned as a proof layer for bounded claims. That is a tension that needs to be resolved: Jev can be a routing signal and a first-pass judge, but it cannot be the terminal verifier for consequential founder-facing claims without an independent check.
Recommendation: Reframe Jev as one interchangeable implementation of a FastDecisionProvider interface, not as "the default fast layer." The estate should support Jev, Clef, Luna, Strands Decider, and local models behind the same contract. The local-decider project already demonstrates that the decider can be swapped by configuration rather than code, with the same HTTP contract across hosted Jev and a local model. This makes the estate decision-model-agnostic and future-proof against the commoditization of the fast-decision layer.

🧩 Missing Pieces in the Whole Picture
1. No distinction between "efficiency" and "effectiveness" at the task level. The document optimizes tokens per task, but the estate's yield requirement defines yield as verified outcome per resource consumed. A 33–82% cost saving with a 16–21% quality reduction (as CostGuard reports) may be a yield loss, not a gain, if the quality reduction crosses the verification threshold. The budget algebra needs a yield floor: a task is not "efficient" if it consumes fewer tokens but fails verification or requires rework. Efficiency must be measured per verified outcome, not per model call.
2. The deterministic gate is under-specified as an organizational artifact. The document describes the gate's logic but not its ownership and evolution. Who maintains the registry of deterministic handlers? How does a handler get promoted from "experimental" to "default"? What happens when a handler's output distribution drifts and it silently becomes non-deterministic? The document's own open question acknowledges this: "How does the estate detect that a 'deterministic' handler has silently become non-deterministic?" That question needs an operational answer—replay tests, output hashing, and a promotion pipeline—before the gate can be trusted at scale.
3. Proof artifacts are described but not standardized. The document specifies a proof record format, but the broader ecosystem is converging on standardized evidence frameworks: the Standardized Evidence Framework (SEF) provides a machine-readable, cryptographically verifiable JSON schema for auditable AI events; Veridict provides an append-only, hash-chained evidence ledger with signed certificates that anyone can verify offline, with a tiered evidence ladder where machine-checkable evidence outranks statistical evidence and jury belief. The estate should adopt or align with an existing standard rather than inventing a proprietary proof format. A proprietary format creates interoperability friction; an aligned standard makes the estate's proofs portable and independently verifiable, which is precisely what the founder-facing proof requirement demands.
4. Calibration under distribution shift is acknowledged as an open problem but not operationalized. The document correctly notes that calibration under distribution shift remains an open problem. The research landscape offers concrete mechanisms: Adaptive Conformal Inference (ACI) dynamically adjusts routing thresholds while maintaining formal risk bounds under distribution shift; RACER uses conformal risk control and "learn-then-test" calibration to provide finite-sample guarantees on selective risk, achieving 26–45% compute savings at matched error rates and stable with calibration set sizes of n ≥ 150. The estate should not treat calibration as a one-time setup; it should treat it as a continuous control loop with conformal guarantees where the task class permits.
5. The semantic cache's regret bound is cited but not integrated into the budget algebra. The continuous semantic caching framework proves a sublinear regret bound against an optimal oracle in continuous query space, which is a genuine mathematical guarantee. But the document does not specify how this bound is monitored, what happens when empirical regret exceeds the theoretical bound, or how cache switching costs interact with the budget. The cache is treated as a savings mechanism, not as a risk-bearing component with its own failure modes and guarantees. It needs a cache governance policy: threshold calibration to a maximum error rate, regret monitoring, and automatic invalidation when the bound is violated.

⚡ Tensions Between the Token-Efficiency Architecture and the Broader Estate
Tension 1: Proof vs. efficiency. The estate's first principle is "no founder response without machine-verified proof." Token efficiency's first principle is "do not call a model unless deterministic options are exhausted." These are aligned in spirit—both refuse to pay for unverified output—but they collide in practice when verification itself is expensive. Formal verification can be token-expensive; the document acknowledges Anthropic's formal-verification run reportedly xaexceeding 100 million tokens. The budget algebra includes B_verify, but the document does not specify how to trade verification cost against model-call savings. A task that skips a model call but requires an expensive formal proof may be less efficient per verified outcome than a task that calls a cheap model and passes a lightweight check. The efficiency metric needs to include verification cost in the denominator, not treat it as an externality.
Tension 2: Jev's speed vs. the proof requirement. Jev resolves bounded decisions in ~102 ms at $0.042 per million input tokens. That makes it an attractive gate—a fast filter that escalates ambiguous cases to slower, more expensive verification. But the document also assigns Jev a role in the proof layer: "Jev is the fast proof layer for bounded claims." These two roles are not the same. A gate can be wrong occasionally if its errors are caught downstream; a proof cannot be wrong. If Jev is used as a proof layer without an independent verifier, the proof architecture is compromised. The resolution is to separate Jev-as-gate (fast, permissive, escalates uncertainty) from Jev-as-evidence (one signal among several, never the sole verifier for consequential claims).
Tension 3: Founder override vs. mathematical enforcement. The document's override design is thoughtful—scoped, TTL-bound, logged, cannot bypass proof—but it sits uneasily with the mathematical enforcement claim. A hard budget is only hard if it cannot be overridden arbitrarily. If the founder can raise ε, extend a TTL, or switch a task class to advisory mode, the "mathematical guarantee" is conditional on the founder's restraint. That is not necessarily a flaw—founders must be able to override—but the document should be precise about what is mathematically guaranteed (the gateway enforces tokens_billed ≤ B_T when no override is active) versus what is policy-guaranteed (the override is time-bound and logged). The distinction matters for external audit: an auditor will ask what happens when the founder is wrong.

🗺️ Integration: Where Token Efficiency Sits in the Full Estate
The token-efficiency architecture is the resource governor for three other estate systems:


Estate system	How token efficiency integrates
Decision-as-a-Service	The deterministic gate and Jev (or a substitute fast-decision provider) are the first stage of every decision pipeline. Only decisions that survive the gate and the fast layer reach a generative model. This is the practical implementation of "map → reduce": map the decision to a deterministic or fast-decision handler, reduce to a model call only when necessary.
Proof and yield	B_verify is a first-class budget line. Yield is measured as verified outcomes per total resource consumed, including verification. A task that saves tokens but fails verification has negative yield, not positive efficiency. Proof artifacts should align with SEF or Veridict-style standards so they are independently verifiable and portable.
Living memory and learning	Every gate decision, cache hit, escalation, override, and budget exhaustion is a learning signal. The estate should track which deterministic handlers resolve which task classes, which Jev-style provider performs best per class, which compression fidelity floors hold, and where calibration drifts. This is the "learn what not to do" requirement applied to the efficiency stack itself.
🚀 Recommended Roadmap Adjustment
The document's four-phase implementation sequence is reasonable, but it needs three corrections to survive the whole picture:
Phase 1 (corrected): Deterministic gate + provider-agnostic fast layer + standardized proof artifact.
* Build the deterministic handler registry and pre-model gate, with replay-based drift detection for handlers.
* Define a FastDecisionProvider interface and implement at least two backends (Jev and one alternative: Clef, a local model, or Strands Decider) from day one. Do not hard-code Jev.
* Adopt or align with an existing proof artifact standard (SEF or Veridict) rather than inventing a proprietary format.
Phase 2 (corrected): Efficiency per verified outcome, not per call.
* Extend the ET metric to include B_verify and rework cost. A task is only "efficient" if it passes verification within its total budget.
* Implement cache governance with regret monitoring and automatic invalidation.
* Operationalize calibration as a continuous loop with conformal risk control where applicable.
Phase 3 (corrected): Conditional guarantees and override accounting.
* Separate the mathematical guarantee (gateway-enforced cap when no override is active) from the policy guarantee (override is time-bound and logged). Expose this distinction to auditors.
* Implement founder override as a budgeted action: every override consumes an override budget, and repeated overrides trigger a review rather than silently becoming permanent.
Phase 4: Future-proofing through modularity and protocol generation.
* Treat the fast-decision layer, router, verifier, compressor, and cache as replaceable modules with versioned contracts. Re-benchmark on every model, tokenizer, or provider change.
* Generate efficiency protocols per problem class that specify which deterministic handlers exist, which fast-decision provider is approved, which cache thresholds apply, and which fidelity floor is allowed. These protocols become part of the estate's living memory and are subject to the same regression gates as code.

The whole picture is a control-plane architecture for economical, provable work. Its weakest point is not the budget algebra or the deterministic gate—those are sound and well-supported. Its weakest point is the assumption that Jev is the stable center of the fast layer. The decision-model market is moving faster than any single provider can be trusted as a permanent dependency. The estate should own the gate, the proof, and the governance—and treat the decision model itself as a replaceable component behind a stable interface. That is the architecture that survives the commodity race.
